WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.140
"The Flash" is changing everything&nbsp;
for the DC cinematic universe – but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.620
what exactly happens next? Here's&nbsp;
where things stand for the series now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.459
that the credits have rolled. Warning:&nbsp;
major spoilers ahead for "The Flash."

00:00:11.459 --> 00:00:13.788
"Don't let your tragedy define you."

00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:18.180
"What did you do?!"

00:00:18.615 --> 00:00:22.500
No matter how many cameos and Bat-folks&nbsp;
"The Flash" features, it's ultimately a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.940
movie about two things: Barry Allen's personal&nbsp;
development, and the butterfly effect of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.260
travel. While exploring these things, the film&nbsp;
introduces a whole lot of new concepts, as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.460
as a number of intriguing alternate-universe&nbsp;
characters, some of whom are extremely familiar.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.440
The movie's climax more or less confirms that&nbsp;
every major DC-adjacent project — from the Tim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.000
Burton "Superman" movie that never existed&nbsp;
to the 1960s "Batman" show — takes place in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.883
some alternate universe. In fact, the bulk&nbsp;
of "The Flash" seems to take place in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:53.460
Burton "Batman" universe, where Michael Keaton's&nbsp;
Batman is the only high-profile costumed hero.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.080
Then again, "The Flash" is also the second to last&nbsp;
movie in the DC Extended Universe, which is set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.720
to give way to the new James Gunn &amp; Peter Safran&nbsp;
DCU after "Aquaman and the Lost Kingdom." So, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:09.120
exactly is its impact on the DC movie universe's&nbsp;
future? Is George Clooney the main Batman again?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.376 --> 00:01:13.756
What does Aquaman do when he wakes up in the&nbsp;
puddle Barry leaves him in the post-credits scene?

00:01:13.756 --> 00:01:15.660
Will the Flash himself appear in more movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.920
or have we seen the last of him? Few of&nbsp;
these questions have concrete answers yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.640
but based on what we know about the franchise's&nbsp;
future, here's what seems most likely.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.720
The big thing that determines much of "The&nbsp;
Flash's" impact on the DC movie universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.040
is whether the movie is considered a part of&nbsp;
the DCU. Right now, it doesn't appear to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.320
Of the four DC movies that were in production&nbsp;
when Gunn became the DC Studios co-president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.109
the only one he's confirmed to be&nbsp;
in the DCU is "Blue Beetle." The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:44.934
filmmaker shared as much in an appearance&nbsp;
on Inside of You with Michael Rosenbaum.

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:51.480
"The first DCU character for sure is Blue Beetle,&nbsp;
and the first full DCU movie is 'Superman.'"

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.820
Going by this logic, this would make "The&nbsp;
Flash" an "Elseworlds" story that effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.360
takes place in its own bubble universe,&nbsp;
much like the ones we see in its climax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.620
The events of the movie certainly happen —&nbsp;
they're just not part of the mainline DCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.217
and therefore wacky things like George Clooney&nbsp;
returning as Bruce Wayne can take place.

00:02:09.217 --> 00:02:13.800
Of course, all of this can change at a moment's&nbsp;
notice. Gunn could fire up his Twitter tomorrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.240
and announce that "The Flash" is a DCU movie,&nbsp;
which would mean that its multiverse concepts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.900
time-traveling antics, and Clooney Bruce&nbsp;
Waynes are all legitimate parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.142
mainline story ... at least until Barry&nbsp;
takes another jog into the chrono bowl.

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:31.020
As for the Flash himself, the character is such&nbsp;
a key DC hero that it would be frankly shocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.580
if a version of him wouldn't make an appearance in&nbsp;
the DCU at some point down the line. It remains to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.600
be seen whether "The Flash" will get a sequel&nbsp;
and if the controversy-riddled Miller will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.340
recast if it happens, but Barry Allen could still&nbsp;
have a future in the DCU if "The Flash" performs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:48.240
at the box office. After all, Gunn has said&nbsp;
that while the movie is something of a reset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.440
it is by no means a hard reset. Gunn&nbsp;
previously shared on social media,

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.720
"Flash resets many things, not all things.&nbsp;
Some characters remain the same, some do not."

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.160
"The Flash" is an ideas movie, and the&nbsp;
"reset" Gunn mentions is quite likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.240
the introduction of the multiverse — not so&nbsp;
much the alternate-universes concept itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.268
but the opportunity it provides&nbsp;
to mix and match characters.

00:03:09.268 --> 00:03:13.620
"The Flash" has plenty of fun doing this, and it&nbsp;
proves that the concept can work pretty well. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.600
teaming Tim Burton-era Batman with two different&nbsp;
Barry Allens and franchise newcomer Kara Zor-El&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.280
and putting them against "Man of Steel" villain&nbsp;
General Zod, the movie employs a fun "smashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.660
different action figures together" strategy that&nbsp;
seems like a great fit for Gunn. The introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.740
of this concept may be "The Flash's" true&nbsp;
legacy in the long run, as it acts as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.940
precedent that gives the DCU free rein to&nbsp;
use old and new characters as they see fit.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:41.340
Combined with the "Elseworlds" concept, this means&nbsp;
that the DCU can now operate ... however it likes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.144
really, which is probably just how Gunn and Safran&nbsp;
like it. If they want to roll with Jason Momoa's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.144 --> 00:03:50.100
Aquaman going forward, they can totally keep him&nbsp;
and bring him in the next Justice League with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.540
whoever ends up playing the new mainline versions&nbsp;
of Superman, Batman, Wonder Woman, and so forth.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.580
On the other hand, if they want to oust an actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.480
"The Flash's" endless amount of alternate&nbsp;
universes and changing futures have given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.440
the DCU a perfect toolkit to replace the&nbsp;
characters with new versions — which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.966
come to think of it, is pretty much what&nbsp;
the movie does to Ben Affleck's Batman.

00:04:07.966 --> 00:04:11.280
It's hard to believe that the DCU&nbsp;
wouldn't establish a mainline universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.360
because it'll probably need a throughline for&nbsp;
longer arcs, and people will likely want to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.720
what the "main" versions of characters&nbsp;
are to truly care about them. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.640
even with this very minor limitation, it's easy to&nbsp;
see why Gunn has praised "The Flash." After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.140
the movie has given his DCU the ultimate&nbsp;
gift: near-total creative freedom.

